# LAN IP + gateway port of the machine running the backend (no trailing slash).
# All 13 services now sit behind one nginx gateway (Backend/docker-compose.yml,
# port 8080 by default per scripts/dev-up.sh) instead of each having its own port.
# The app runs on a physical phone, so `localhost` will NOT resolve to your dev
# machine — you must use its LAN IP. Find it with:
#   macOS:   ipconfig getifaddr en0
#   Windows: ipconfig  (look for IPv4 Address on your active adapter)
# Update this whenever your Wi-Fi network or IP changes.
# NOTE: unlike the old Expo/Metro setup, changing this requires a native rebuild —
# a Metro restart alone will not pick up the new value (Android: re-run `npm run
# android`, sometimes `cd android && ./gradlew clean` first; iOS: re-run `pod install`
# then `npm run ios`).
#
# WARNING — iOS App Transport Security (ATS) gotcha:
# iOS blocks plain-http connections to IPs in the carrier-grade-NAT range
# 100.64.0.0/10 (100.64.0.0-100.127.255.255) unless you add an ATS exception —
# a physical iPhone will fail every request with no useful error. If
# `ipconfig getifaddr en0` (or your VPN/tethering setup) hands you an address
# in that range, it will NOT work for iOS testing; use your machine's real LAN
# IP instead (typically 192.168.x.x or 10.x.x.x). Android is not affected by ATS.
#
# WARNING — API_HOST is only for local dev against the nginx gateway directly.
# The release-build fallback (PROD_HOST in src/config/services.ts) points at
# https://apps.vapko-ti.com/csn, which assumes an outer reverse proxy in front
# of nginx that adds/strips the `/csn` prefix — nginx itself (Backend/nginx/
# nginx.conf) has no `/csn` locations and will 404 without that proxy. Do not
# expect a release build to work against production until that proxy exists.
API_HOST=http://192.168.1.41:8080
